import Sheaf.RingTheory.SingularIdeal
import Mathlib.RingTheory.FinitePresentation

/-! # Popescu lifting lemma

Source: Stacks Project, Smoothing Ring Maps, Lemma 07CP
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-popescu-lifting).
-/

universe a b c

namespace Algebra

/-- Popescu's lifting lemma. The quotient maps in the conclusion are specified by their
composites with the surjective quotient projections; thus they are the induced maps.
The last smoothness clause uses the algebra structure induced by `cmod` on `D / πD`.

Source: Stacks Project, Smoothing Ring Maps, Lemma 07CP
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-popescu-lifting).

Proof (the eighteen steps of the DAG node):
1. (Presentation; Noetherianity.) Since $\bar C$ is of finite presentation over $R/\pi^2R$ [def-
weil-pbc-finitely-presented-algebra], choose a presentation $\bar
C\cong(R/\pi^2R)[x_1,\dots,x_n]/(\bar f_1,\dots,\bar f_m)$ and lifts $f_j\in
P:=R[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ of the $\bar f_j$. Put $\bar P=(R/\pi^2R)[x_1,\dots,x_n]=P/\pi^2P$,
$I=(f_1,\dots,f_m)\subseteq P$, $\bar I=(\bar f_1,\dots,\bar f_m)\subseteq\bar P$ its image, and
for $E\subseteq\{1,\dots,m\}$, $I_E=(f_j,\ j\in E)$ with image $\bar I_E\subseteq\bar P$. Thus
$\bar C=\bar P/\bar I=P/(I+\pi^2P)$; we write $x_i$ also for the images of the variables, and
$\bar a$ for the image in $\bar C$ of $a\in P$. $\bar C$ is a finitely generated algebra over
the Noetherian ring $R$ [def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring], hence Noetherian [lem-weil-pbc-finite-
type-algebra-noetherian]. Let $W\subseteq\operatorname{Spec}\bar C$ be the set of primes at
which $R/\pi^2R\to\bar C$ is smooth [def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal]. Dependencies: def-weil-pbc-
finitely-presented-algebra, def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring, lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-
noetherian, def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal.

2. (Finitely many good elements: Stacks (1), (2).) For each $\bar{\mathfrak q}\in W$ apply [lem-
weil-pbc-smooth-at-prime-free-conormal-basis] to the ring $R/\pi^2R$, the presentation $\bar
C=\bar P/\bar I$ and $\bar{\mathfrak q}$: there are $a_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}\in P$ (lift an
element of $\bar P$) with $\bar a_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}\notin\bar{\mathfrak q}$ and
$V_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}\subseteq\{1,\dots,m\}$ such that $\bar C_{\bar a}$ is smooth over
$R/\pi^2R$, $(\bar I/\bar I^2)_{\bar a}$ is free over $\bar C_{\bar a}$ on the classes of $\bar
f_j$, $j\in V_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}$, and $\Omega_{\bar C_{\bar a}/(R/\pi^2R)}$ is free of rank
$n-|V_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}|$ ($a=a_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}$). Let $T\subseteq\bar C$ be the set of
all $\bar a_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}$. The ideal $(T)$ is finitely generated ($\bar C$ Noetherian
[def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring]); each of finitely many generators is a combination of finitely
many elements of $T$, so $(T)=(t_1,\dots,t_r)$ with $t_k\in T$. Choose for each $k$ one
$\bar{\mathfrak q}$ with $t_k=\bar a_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}$ and put $a_k=a_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}$,
$E_k=V_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}$. Then: (1) $W=\bigcup_kD(\bar a_k)$: a prime $\bar{\mathfrak q}\in
W$ does not contain $\bar a_{\bar{\mathfrak q}}\in(T)$, so it misses some $t_k$; conversely
$\bar C_{\bar a_k}$ smooth gives $D(\bar a_k)\subseteq W$ [def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal]; (2)
$(\bar I/\bar I^2)_{\bar a_k}$ is free over $\bar C_{\bar a_k}$ on the classes of $\bar f_j$,
$j\in E_k$; (2') $\bar C_{\bar a_k}$ is smooth over $R/\pi^2R$ and $\Omega_{\bar C_{\bar
a_k}/(R/\pi^2R)}$ is free of rank $n-|E_k|$. Properties (1), (2), (2') only depend on $D(\bar
a_k)$ and the ring $\bar C_{\bar a_k}$; so they persist when $a_k$ is replaced by $a'_k\in P$
with $\bar a'_k=\bar a_k^{s}$ for some $s\ge1$ (then $\bar C_{\bar a'_k}=\bar C_{\bar a_k}$).
Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-smooth-at-prime-free-conormal-basis, def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring,
def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal, def-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map, def-weil-pbc-module-localization-
away.

3. (Nakayama: Stacks (3).) Fix $k$. In $\bar P_{a_k}$ let $J=\bar I\bar P_{a_k}$ and $M=(\bar
I/\bar I_{E_k})_{a_k}$, a finitely generated $\bar P_{a_k}$-module. By (2) the localization of
$\bar I/\bar I^2$ at $a_k$ is generated by the classes of $\bar f_j$, $j\in E_k$, i.e. $\bar
I_{a_k}=(\bar I_{E_k})_{a_k}+(\bar I^2)_{a_k}$ (localization is exact [def-weil-pbc-module-
localization-away]), hence $M=JM$. By [lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-one-plus-ideal-annihilator] there
is $e\in\bar P_{a_k}$ with $e-1\in J$ and $eM=0$. Write $e-1=\bar b/a_k^N$ with $b\in I$ (lift
the numerator from $\bar I$ to $I$), so $e=\overline{(a_k^N+b)}/a_k^N$. Replace $a_k$ by
$a'_k=a_k(a_k^N+b)$: since $\bar b=0$ in $\bar C$, $\bar a'_k=\bar a_k^{N+1}$, so (1), (2), (2')
persist (Step 2); and $(\bar I/\bar I_{E_k})_{a'_k}$ is a localization of $M$ in which the
annihilator $a_k^Ne=\overline{a_k^N+b}$ of $M$ is a unit, so it is $0$. This gives (3): $(\bar
I_{E_k})_{a_k}=\bar I_{a_k}$ in $\bar P_{a_k}$. (Stacks says 'replacing $a_k$ by $a_kb_k$'; with
$b_k\in I$ that element maps to $0$ in $\bar C$, so the intended replacement is
$a_k(a_k^N+b_k)$, as here.) Dependencies: def-weil-pbc-module-localization-away, lem-weil-pbc-
nakayama-one-plus-ideal-annihilator.

4. (Relations (4).) By (3), for every $k$ and $\ell$, $\bar f_\ell/1\in(\bar I_{E_k})_{a_k}$,
i.e. $\bar f_\ell/1=\sum_{j\in E_k}(\bar y_j/a_k^s)\bar f_j$ in $\bar P_{a_k}$; equality in
$\bar P_{a_k}$ means equality in $\bar P$ after multiplying by a power of $a_k$, so $a_k^t\bar
f_\ell=\sum_{j\in E_k}\bar h_j\bar f_j$ in $\bar P$ for some $t$ and $\bar h_j\in\bar P$;
lifting to $P$, $a_k^tf_\ell-\sum_{j\in E_k}h_jf_j\in\pi^2P$. Taking one $t$ for all (finitely
many) $k,\ell$ and replacing each $a_k$ by $a_k^t$ (allowed by Step 2; (3) persists as $\bar
P_{a_k^t}=\bar P_{a_k}$) we obtain $h^j_{k,\ell},g_{k,\ell}\in P$ with (4) $a_kf_\ell=\sum_{j\in
E_k}h^j_{k,\ell}f_j+\pi^2g_{k,\ell}$ for all $k\in\{1,\dots,r\}$, $\ell\in\{1,\dots,m\}$; for
$\ell\in E_k$ we take instead $h^j_{k,\ell}=a_k\delta_{\ell j}$ and $g_{k,\ell}=0$, for which
(4) holds trivially. Dependencies: .

5. (The algebra $D$; property (a).) Let $Q=R[x_1,\dots,x_n,z_1,\dots,z_m]$,
$p_{k,\ell}=a_kz_\ell-\sum_{j\in E_k}h^j_{k,\ell}z_j-\pi g_{k,\ell}\in Q$ (so $p_{k,\ell}=0$ for
$\ell\in E_k$) and $D=Q/(f_j-\pi z_j\ (1\le j\le m),\ p_{k,\ell}\ (1\le k\le r,\,1\le\ell\le
m))$ with $R\to D$ the structure map. Finitely many variables and relations: $D$ is of finite
presentation over $R$ [def-weil-pbc-finitely-presented-algebra], which is (a). Dependencies:
def-weil-pbc-finitely-presented-algebra.

6. (The map $D\to\Lambda$.) Choose $\lambda_i\in\Lambda$ whose class modulo $\pi^2\Lambda$ is
the image of $x_i$ under $\bar C\to\Lambda/\pi^2\Lambda$, and for $f\in P$ let
$f(\lambda)\in\Lambda$ be its image under the $R$-algebra map $P\to\Lambda$,
$x_i\mapsto\lambda_i$. Since $\bar f_j=0$ in $\bar C$, $f_j(\lambda)\in\pi^2\Lambda$:
$f_j(\lambda)=\pi^2\mu_j$ with $\mu_j\in\Lambda$. Let $Q\to\Lambda$ be the $R$-algebra map
$x_i\mapsto\lambda_i$, $z_j\mapsto\pi\mu_j$. It sends $f_j-\pi z_j$ to $0$, and $p_{k,\ell}$ to
$\pi Y$ with $Y=a_k(\lambda)\mu_\ell-\sum_{j\in E_k}h^j_{k,\ell}(\lambda)\mu_j-
g_{k,\ell}(\lambda)$; by (4) evaluated at $\lambda$, $\pi^2Y=a_k(\lambda)f_\ell(\lambda)-
\sum_jh^j_{k,\ell}(\lambda)f_j(\lambda)-\pi^2g_{k,\ell}(\lambda)=0$, so
$Y\in\mathrm{Ann}_\Lambda(\pi^2)=\mathrm{Ann}_\Lambda(\pi)$ and $\pi Y=0$. Hence $Q\to\Lambda$
factors through an $R$-algebra map $\psi:D\to\Lambda$. Dependencies: .

7. (The map $\bar C\to D/\pi D$ and the diagram.) The $R$-algebra map $P\to D/\pi D$,
$x_i\mapsto x_i$, kills $\pi^2$ and each $f_j=(f_j-\pi z_j)+\pi z_j$, hence factors through
$\bar C=P/(I+\pi^2P)$: an $R/\pi^2R$-algebra map $\rho:\bar C\to D/\pi D$. The left square
commutes because both composites $R/\pi^2R\to D/\pi D$ are $R$-algebra maps. The right square
commutes because $\bar C\to\Lambda/\pi^2\Lambda\to\Lambda/\pi\Lambda$ and $\bar
C\xrightarrow{\rho}D/\pi D\xrightarrow{\bar\psi}\Lambda/\pi\Lambda$ are $R$-algebra maps from
$\bar C$, which is generated by the $x_i$, both sending $x_i$ to the class of $\lambda_i$.
Dependencies: .

8. (Key equality.) For all $k,\ell$, in $Q$: $\pi p_{k,\ell}=\pi a_kz_\ell-\sum_{j\in E_k}\pi
h^j_{k,\ell}z_j-\pi^2g_{k,\ell}=-a_k(f_\ell-\pi z_\ell)+\sum_{j\in E_k}h^j_{k,\ell}(f_j-\pi
z_j)$, by substituting $\pi^2g_{k,\ell}=a_kf_\ell-\sum_jh^j_{k,\ell}f_j$ from (4). Dependencies:
.

9. (Property (b).) Invert $\pi$. By Step 8 every $p_{k,\ell}$ lies in the ideal of $Q_\pi$
generated by the $f_j-\pi z_j=-\pi(z_j-\pi^{-1}f_j)$, so $D_\pi=Q_\pi/(z_j-\pi^{-1}f_j,\ 1\le
j\le m)\cong P_\pi=R_\pi[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ as $R$-algebras ($z_j\mapsto\pi^{-1}f_j$). $R\to P$ is
smooth [lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-smooth], hence $R\to P_\pi$ is smooth [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-
ring-map-principal-localization] [def-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map], so $D_\pi$ is a smooth
$R$-algebra. For a prime $\mathfrak q\subset D$ with $\pi\notin\mathfrak q$ the element
$g=\pi\notin\mathfrak q$ shows that $R\to D$ is smooth at $\mathfrak q$ [def-weil-pbc-singular-
ideal]: (b). Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-smooth, lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-
principal-localization, def-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map, def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal.

10. (The rings $D_k$.) Fix $k$ and let $D_k=Q/(f_j-\pi z_j\ (j\in E_k),\ p_{k,\ell}\ (\ell\notin
E_k))$: $n+m$ variables and $|E_k|+(m-|E_k|)=m$ relations (the $p_{k,\ell}$ with $\ell\in E_k$
are $0$). The relations of $D_k$ are among those of $D$, so $D=D_k/J_k$ where $J_k\subseteq D_k$
is the ideal generated by the images of $f_\ell-\pi z_\ell$ ($\ell\notin E_k$) and of
$p_{k',\ell}$ ($k'\ne k$, all $\ell$), a finitely generated ideal. Dependencies: .

11. (The ring $(D_k/\pi D_k)_{a_k}$.) Write $\tilde f$ for the image of $f$ in $(R/\pi R)[x,z]$.
By (4) modulo $\pi$, $a_k\tilde f_\ell\in(\tilde f_j,\ j\in E_k)$ for all $\ell$, so in $(R/\pi
R)[x]_{a_k}$ the ideals $(\tilde f_j,\ j\in E_k)$ and $(\tilde f_1,\dots,\tilde f_m)$ agree and
$(R/\pi R)[x]_{a_k}/(\tilde f_j,\ j\in E_k)=(\bar C/\pi\bar C)_{a_k}$ (as $\bar C/\pi\bar
C=(R/\pi R)[x]/(\tilde f_1,\dots,\tilde f_m)$). Hence $(D_k/\pi D_k)_{a_k}=(\bar C/\pi\bar
C)_{a_k}[z_1,\dots,z_m]/(a_kz_\ell-\sum_{j\in E_k}h^j_{k,\ell}z_j,\ \ell\notin E_k)\cong(\bar
C/\pi\bar C)_{a_k}[z_j,\ j\in E_k]$, solving for $z_\ell=a_k^{-1}\sum_jh^j_{k,\ell}z_j$ ($a_k$
is a unit). This is an isomorphism of $\bar C/\pi\bar C$-algebras for the map $\bar C/\pi\bar
C\to(D_k/\pi D_k)_{a_k}$, $x_i\mapsto x_i$, which is well defined by the same computation and is
compatible with $\rho$ (Step 7) under $D_k\to D$. Dependencies: .

12. (Smoothness modulo $\pi$.) By (2'), $(\bar C/\pi\bar C)_{a_k}\cong(R/\pi
R)\otimes_{R/\pi^2R}\bar C_{\bar a_k}$ is smooth over $R/\pi R$ [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-
base-change-composition] (1), with $\Omega_{(\bar C/\pi\bar C)_{a_k}/(R/\pi R)}$ free of rank
$n-|E_k|$ [lem-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials-base-change]. By Step 11, $(D_k/\pi D_k)_{a_k}$ is
a polynomial ring in $|E_k|$ variables over it, hence smooth over it [lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-
ring-smooth], hence smooth over $R/\pi R$ (composition (2)), and $\Omega_{(D_k/\pi
D_k)_{a_k}/(R/\pi R)}$ is free of rank $(n-|E_k|)+|E_k|=n$ [lem-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials-
polynomial-extension]. Moreover $\bar C/\pi\bar C\to(\bar C/\pi\bar C)_{a_k}$ is smooth (the
identity of $\bar C/\pi\bar C$ is smooth, a polynomial ring in $0$ variables, and [lem-weil-pbc-
smooth-ring-map-principal-localization]), so $\bar C/\pi\bar C\to(D_k/\pi D_k)_{a_k}$ is smooth
(composition), i.e. $\bar C/\pi\bar C\to D_k/\pi D_k$ is smooth at every prime not containing
$a_k$ [def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal] [def-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map]. Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-
smooth-ring-map-base-change-composition, lem-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials-base-change, lem-
weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-smooth, lem-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials-polynomial-extension, lem-
weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-principal-localization, def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal, def-weil-pbc-
smooth-ring-map.

13. (The fibre of $R\to D_k$ at $\mathfrak q_k$ is smooth of dimension $n$.) Let $\mathfrak
q_k\subset D_k$ be a prime with $\pi\in\mathfrak q_k$ and $a_k\notin\mathfrak q_k$, $\mathfrak
p=\mathfrak q_k\cap R$ (so $\pi\in\mathfrak p$), $F=D_k\otimes_R\kappa(\mathfrak p)$ the fibre
ring [def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime] and $\mathfrak Q_k\subset F$ the kernel of
$F\to\kappa(\mathfrak q_k)$. As $\pi\mapsto0$ in $\kappa(\mathfrak p)$, $F\cong(D_k/\pi
D_k)\otimes_{R/\pi R}\kappa(\mathfrak p)$ and $F_{a_k}\cong(D_k/\pi D_k)_{a_k}\otimes_{R/\pi
R}\kappa(\mathfrak p)$; by Step 12 it is smooth over $\kappa(\mathfrak p)$ [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-
ring-map-base-change-composition] with $\Omega_{F_{a_k}/\kappa(\mathfrak p)}$ free of rank $n$
[lem-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials-base-change]. Since $a_k$ maps to a unit of $\kappa(\mathfrak
q_k)$, $a_k\notin\mathfrak Q_k$, so $\kappa(\mathfrak p)\to F$ is smooth at $\mathfrak Q_k$
[def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal], $F$ is finitely generated over $\kappa(\mathfrak p)$, and
$\Omega_{F/\kappa(\mathfrak p)}\otimes_F\kappa(\mathfrak
Q_k)\cong\Omega_{F_{a_k}/\kappa(\mathfrak p)}\otimes\kappa(\mathfrak Q_k)$ [lem-weil-pbc-kahler-
differentials-localization] has dimension $n$. By [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-over-field-dimension-
differentials] the dimension of $\operatorname{Spec}F$ at $\mathfrak Q_k$ is $n$. Dependencies:
def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime, lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-base-change-composition, lem-
weil-pbc-kahler-differentials-base-change, def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal, lem-weil-pbc-kahler-
differentials-localization, lem-weil-pbc-smooth-over-field-dimension-differentials.

14. ($R\to D_k$ is smooth at $\mathfrak q_k$.) For $\mathfrak q_k$ as in Step 13: $D_k$ is
$R[n+m\text{ variables}]$ modulo $m$ relations (Step 10) and the fibre has dimension $n=(n+m)-m$
at $\mathfrak Q_k$, so by [lem-weil-pbc-equation-count-fibre-dimension-flat] the local ring map
$R_{\mathfrak p}\to(D_k)_{\mathfrak q_k}$ is flat ('syntomic at $\mathfrak q_k$'). $D_k$ is of
finite presentation over $R$ [def-weil-pbc-finitely-presented-algebra] and $\kappa(\mathfrak
p)\to F$ is smooth at $\mathfrak Q_k$ (Step 13), so by [lem-weil-pbc-flat-smooth-fibre-smooth-
at-prime] $R\to D_k$ is smooth at $\mathfrak q_k$. Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-equation-count-
fibre-dimension-flat, def-weil-pbc-finitely-presented-algebra, lem-weil-pbc-flat-smooth-fibre-
smooth-at-prime.

15. (The relations $f_\ell-\pi z_\ell$ vanish on $D(a_k)$.) In $D_k$ we have $p_{k,\ell}=0$ and
$f_j-\pi z_j=0$ for $j\in E_k$, so the key equality (Step 8) gives $a_k(f_\ell-\pi z_\ell)=0$ in
$D_k$; as $a_k$ is a unit in $(D_k)_{a_k}$, $f_\ell-\pi z_\ell=0$ in $(D_k)_{a_k}$ for every
$\ell$. Dependencies: .

16. (The $p_{k',\ell}$ lie in $\pi(D_k)_{a_k}$.) Let $k',\ell$ be given and
$c_j=a_{k'}h^j_{k,\ell}-\sum_{j'\in E_{k'}}h^{j'}_{k',\ell}h^j_{k,j'}\in P$ for $j\in E_k$. In
the $\bar C$-module $\bar I/\bar I^2$, (4) gives $a_k\bar f_\ell=\sum_{j\in E_k}h^j_{k,\ell}\bar
f_j$ (and likewise for $k'$); computing $a_{k'}a_k\bar f_\ell$ once as $a_{k'}\sum_{j\in
E_k}h^j_{k,\ell}\bar f_j$ and once as $a_k\sum_{j'\in E_{k'}}h^{j'}_{k',\ell}\bar
f_{j'}=\sum_{j'\in E_{k'}}h^{j'}_{k',\ell}\sum_{j\in E_k}h^j_{k,j'}\bar f_j$ gives $\sum_{j\in
E_k}c_j\bar f_j=0$ in $\bar I/\bar I^2$. By (2) every $c_j$ is $0$ in $\bar C_{\bar a_k}$, i.e.
$a_k^{N_1}c_j\in I+\pi^2P$; by (3) and finite generation of $\bar I$, $a_k^{N_2}I\subseteq
I_{E_k}+\pi^2P$; so $a_k^Nc_j\in I_{E_k}+\pi^2P$ with $N=N_1+N_2$ [def-weil-pbc-module-
localization-away]. Expanding the definitions, $a_kp_{k',\ell}-a_{k'}p_{k,\ell}+\sum_{j'\in
E_{k'}}h^{j'}_{k',\ell}p_{k,j'}\equiv\sum_{j\in E_k}c_jz_j\pmod{\pi Q}$ (the terms
$a_ka_{k'}z_\ell$ and $a_kh^{j'}_{k',\ell}z_{j'}$ cancel). Multiplying by $a_k^N$:
$a_k^{N+1}p_{k',\ell}$ lies in the ideal of $Q$ generated by $I_{E_k}$, $\pi$ and the
$p_{k,\cdot}$. In $(D_k)_{a_k}$ the $p_{k,\cdot}$ vanish and $f_j=\pi z_j$ for $j\in E_k$, so
$I_{E_k}\subseteq\pi(D_k)_{a_k}$; as $a_k$ is a unit, $p_{k',\ell}\in\pi(D_k)_{a_k}$.
Dependencies: def-weil-pbc-module-localization-away.

17. (The kernel $J_k$ vanishes at $\mathfrak q_k$.) Let $\mathfrak q_k$ be as in Step 13. By the
key equality for $k'$, $\pi p_{k',\ell}=-a_{k'}(f_\ell-\pi z_\ell)+\sum_{j'\in
E_{k'}}h^{j'}_{k',\ell}(f_{j'}-\pi z_{j'})$, which is $0$ in $(D_k)_{a_k}$ by Step 15. With Step
16, $p_{k',\ell}=\pi y$ with $y\in(D_k)_{a_k}$ and $\pi^2y=0$. Pass to $(D_k)_{\mathfrak q_k}$,
a localization of $(D_k)_{a_k}$. By Step 14 there is $g\notin\mathfrak q_k$ with $(D_k)_g$
smooth over $R$ [def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal], hence flat [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-flat];
$(D_k)_{\mathfrak q_k}$ is a localization of $(D_k)_g$, flat over it [lem-weil-pbc-localization-
flat], hence flat over $R$ [lem-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map-comp] [def-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map]. By
[lem-weil-pbc-annihilator-element-flat-base-change], $\mathrm{Ann}_{(D_k)_{\mathfrak
q_k}}(\pi^i)=\mathrm{Ann}_R(\pi^i)(D_k)_{\mathfrak q_k}$ for $i=1,2$, and these agree since
$\mathrm{Ann}_R(\pi)=\mathrm{Ann}_R(\pi^2)$. So $\pi y=0$, i.e. $p_{k',\ell}\mapsto0$ in
$(D_k)_{\mathfrak q_k}$. Together with Step 15 all generators of $J_k$ (Step 10) vanish in
$(D_k)_{\mathfrak q_k}$: $(J_k)_{\mathfrak q_k}=0$. Dependencies: def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal,
lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-flat, lem-weil-pbc-localization-flat, lem-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map-
comp, def-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map, lem-weil-pbc-annihilator-element-flat-base-change.

18. (Properties (c) and (d).) Let $\mathfrak q\subset D$ be a prime with $\pi\in\mathfrak q$
such that the prime $\bar{\mathfrak q}=\rho^{-1}(\mathfrak q/\pi D)$ of $\bar C$ lies in $W$. By
(1) some $\bar a_k\notin\bar{\mathfrak q}$; as $\rho(\bar a_k)$ is the class of $a_k$,
$a_k\notin\mathfrak q$. Let $\mathfrak q_k\subset D_k$ be the inverse image of $\mathfrak q$
under $D_k\to D=D_k/J_k$; then $\pi\in\mathfrak q_k$, $a_k\notin\mathfrak q_k$. By Step 17 and
[lem-weil-pbc-finite-module-localization-zero-annihilator] (for the finitely generated
$D_k$-module $J_k$ and $D_k\setminus\mathfrak q_k$) there is $s\in D_k\setminus\mathfrak q_k$
with $sJ_k=0$, so $(D_k)_s\to D_s$ is an isomorphism. By Step 14 there is $g\notin\mathfrak q_k$
with $(D_k)_g$ smooth over $R$; then $D_{sg}\cong(D_k)_{sg}$ is smooth over $R$ [lem-weil-pbc-
smooth-ring-map-principal-localization] and the image of $sg$ is not in $\mathfrak q$: $R\to D$
is smooth at $\mathfrak q$ [def-weil-pbc-singular-ideal], which is (c). Likewise $(D/\pi
D)_{sa_k}\cong(D_k/\pi D_k)_{sa_k}$ is a localization of $(D_k/\pi D_k)_{a_k}$, smooth over
$\bar C/\pi\bar C$ by Step 12 and [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-principal-localization], the
$\bar C/\pi\bar C$-algebra structures agreeing ($x_i\mapsto x_i$, Steps 7 and 11); so $\bar
C/\pi\bar C\to D/\pi D$ is smooth at $\mathfrak q/\pi D$, which is (d) (every prime of $D/\pi D$
is $\mathfrak q/\pi D$ for such a $\mathfrak q$). Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-finite-module-
localization-zero-annihilator, lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-principal-localization, def-weil-
pbc-singular-ideal, def-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map.
-/
theorem popescu_lifting (R : Type a) (Λ : Type b) (Cbar : Type c)
    [CommRing R] [CommRing Λ] [CommRing Cbar]
    [Algebra R Λ] [Algebra R Cbar] [IsNoetherianRing R] (π : R)
    (hAnnR : Module.annihilator R (Ideal.span ({π} : Set R)) =
      Module.annihilator R (Ideal.span ({π ^ 2} : Set R)))
    (hAnnΛ : Module.annihilator Λ
        (Ideal.span ({algebraMap R Λ π} : Set Λ)) =
      Module.annihilator Λ
        (Ideal.span ({algebraMap R Λ (π ^ 2)} : Set Λ)))
    [Algebra (R ⧸ Ideal.span ({π ^ 2} : Set R)) Cbar]
    [IsScalarTower R (R ⧸ Ideal.span ({π ^ 2} : Set R)) Cbar]
    [Algebra.FinitePresentation (R ⧸ Ideal.span ({π ^ 2} : Set R)) Cbar]
    (j : Cbar →ₐ[R] (Λ ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R Λ (π ^ 2)} : Set Λ))) :
    ∃ (D : Type (max a c)) (_ : CommRing D) (_ : Algebra R D)
      (φ : D →ₐ[R] Λ)
      (ψ : (D ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R D π} : Set D)) →+*
        (Λ ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R Λ π} : Set Λ)))
      (red : (Λ ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R Λ (π ^ 2)} : Set Λ)) →+*
        (Λ ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R Λ π} : Set Λ)))
      (c : Cbar →ₐ[R] (D ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R D π} : Set D)))
      (cmod : (Cbar ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R Cbar π} : Set Cbar)) →+*
        (D ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R D π} : Set D))),
      Algebra.FinitePresentation R D ∧
      ψ.comp (Ideal.Quotient.mk _) = (Ideal.Quotient.mk _).comp φ.toRingHom ∧
      red.comp (Ideal.Quotient.mk _) = Ideal.Quotient.mk _ ∧
      ψ.comp c.toRingHom = red.comp j.toRingHom ∧
      cmod.comp (Ideal.Quotient.mk _) = c.toRingHom ∧
      (∀ q : PrimeSpectrum D, algebraMap R D π ∉ q.asIdeal → IsSmoothAtPrime R q) ∧
      (∀ q : PrimeSpectrum (D ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R D π} : Set D)),
        IsSmoothAtPrime (R ⧸ Ideal.span ({π ^ 2} : Set R))
          (PrimeSpectrum.comap c.toRingHom q) →
        IsSmoothAtPrime R (PrimeSpectrum.comap (Ideal.Quotient.mk _) q)) ∧
      (∀ q : PrimeSpectrum (D ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R D π} : Set D)),
        IsSmoothAtPrime (R ⧸ Ideal.span ({π ^ 2} : Set R))
          (PrimeSpectrum.comap c.toRingHom q) →
        @IsSmoothAtPrime (Cbar ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R Cbar π} : Set Cbar))
          (D ⧸ Ideal.span ({algebraMap R D π} : Set D)) _ _ cmod.toAlgebra q) := by
  sorry

end Algebra
